"The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences (OEIS) (https://oeis.org/history?seq=A395495&start=10)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view3\",\"lineno\":20}); Total lines: 262\nL0: 【0†login】\nL1: \nL2: The OEIS is supported by 【1†the many generous donors to the OEIS Foundation†oeisf.org】.\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Winners in the A400K Celebration】\nL5: \nL6: [Input]\nL7: \nL8: [Input]\nL9: \nL10: 【3†Hints】\nL11: \nL12: (Greetings from 【4†The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences】!)\nL13: ## Revision History for 【5†A395495】\nL14: \nL15: (Bold, blue-underlined text is an addition; faded, red-underlined text is a ~~deletion~~.)\nL16: 【6†newer changes】 | Showing entries 11-16\nL17: \nL18: 【5†A395495】\nL19: \nL20: Minimum number of cells in a connected polyomino on the square grid that contains every fixed n-omino as a translated subregion.\nL21: (【7†history】; 【5†published version】)\nL22: \nL23: 【8†#6】 by 【9†Michel Marcus】 at Sun Apr 26 00:26:20 EDT 2026\nL24: \nL25: EXAMPLE\nL26: \nL27: ` . # .`\nL28: \nL29: ` # # #`\nL30: \nL31: ` . # .`\nL32: \nL33: ` . . . # . . .`\nL34: \nL35: ` . . # # # . .`\nL36: \nL37: ` . # . # # # .`\nL38: ` # # # # # # #`\nL39: \nL40: ` . # # # # # .`\nL41: \nL42: ` . . # # # . .`\nL43: \nL44: ` . . . # . . .`\nL45: \nL46: ` . . . . . # . . .`\nL47: \nL48: ` . . . . # # . . .`\nL49: \nL50: ` . . . # # # . . .`\nL51: \nL52: ` . . # # # # # # #`\nL53: \nL54: ` . # # # # # # # #`\nL55: \nL56: ` # # # # # # # # #`\nL57: \nL58: ` . . . # # # . . .`\nL59: \nL60: ` . . . # # # . . .`\nL61: \nL62: ` . . . # # # . . .`\nL63: \nL64: 【10†#5】 by 【9†Michel Marcus】 at Sun Apr 26 00:24:38 EDT 2026\nL65: \nL66: LINKS\nL67: \nL68: `~~_~~Peter Exley~~_~~ ~~,~~ ~~ ~~, <a href=\"https://github.com/pexley-math/oeis-【5†A395495】\">Solver code, data, and figures</a>, GitHub.`\nL69: \nL70: STATUS\nL71: \nL72: `~~proposed~~`\nL73: `editing`\nL74: \nL75: Discussion\nL76: \nL77: Sun Apr 26\nL78: \nL79: 00:24\nL80: \nL81:     Michel Marcus: not here\nL82: \nL83: 【11†#4】 by 【12†Peter Exley】 at Sat Apr 25 23:50:36 EDT 2026\nL84: \nL85: STATUS\nL86: \nL87: `~~editing~~`\nL88: \nL89: `proposed`\nL90: \nL91: 【13†#3】 by 【12†Peter Exley】 at Sat Apr 25 23:49:37 EDT 2026\nL92: \nL93: LINKS\nL94: \nL95: `【14†Peter Exley】, <a href=\"https://github.com/pexley-math/oeis-~~【15†A000000】~~【5†A395495】\">Solver code, data, and figures</a>, GitHub.`\nL96: \nL97: EXAMPLE\nL98: \nL99: `~~The 1 container with bounding box 4 X 4:~~`\nL100: \nL101: `~~. . # .~~`\nL102: \nL103: `~~. # # .~~`\nL104: \nL105: `~~# # # #~~`\nL106: \nL107: `~~. # # .~~`\nL108: `~~The 1 container with bounding box 5 X 5:~~`\nL109: \nL110: `~~. . # . .~~`\nL111: \nL112: `~~. # # # .~~`\nL113: \nL114: `~~# # # # #~~`\nL115: \nL116: `~~. # # # .~~`\nL117: \nL118: `~~. . # . .~~`\nL119: \nL120: `.`\nL121: \nL122: `.`\nL123: \nL124: 【16†#2】 by 【12†Peter Exley】 at Sat Apr 25 20:25:02 EDT 2026\nL125: \nL126: NAME\nL127: \nL128: `~~allocated for Peter Exley~~`\nL129: \nL130: `Minimum number of cells in a connected polyomino on the square grid that contains every fixed n-omino as a translated subregion.`\nL131: \nL132: DATA\nL133: \nL134: `1, 3, 5, 9, 13, 18, 24, 31, 39`\nL135: \nL136: OFFSET\nL137: \nL138: `1,2`\nL139: \nL140: COMMENTS\nL141: `Containment is translation-only: the contained piece may not be rotated or reflected. Number of fixed n-ominoes is 【17†A001168】. Each of a(1) through a(9) is proved via SAT + CEGAR connectivity and carries a DRAT UNSAT certificate at k = a(n) - 1 that is independently verified by drat-trim (verdict s VERIFIED for every n = 1..9). A pure-Python geometric containment verifier also re-confirms that every reported container does contain every fixed n-omino as a translate.`\nL142: `Conjecture (UNVERIFIED): a(n) = (n^2 - n + 6)/2 for n >= 4, with a(n) = 2n - 1 for n in {1, 2, 3}. Equivalently a(n) = 【18†A000217】(n-1) + 3 for n >= 4. Predicts a(10) = 48.`\nL143: `The first nine terms coincide with 【19†A350660】 (rounded average key-comparison count for bubble sort, from Knuth, TAOCP Vol. 3), which arises from a rounded asymptotic expansion and has no combinatorial relationship to polyomino containment. Under the conjecture above the sequences diverge from n = 13: this sequence is conjecturally a(13) = 81 whereas 【19†A350660】(13) = 80, with the 1-cell gap persisting for every larger n listed in 【19†A350660】.\nL144: Proving a(13) directly is beyond current DRAT-certified reach; the distinction is semantic (independent problem statements) rather than only numerical.`\nL145: `At n = 7 every minimum container has a single 1-cell interior hole. SAT model-enumeration over the 7 X 7 bounding box (the natural search box, which must contain both the horizontal and vertical n-cell I-omino) finds exactly four 24-cell containers; all four are the 25-cell discrete L_1 ball of radius 3 (centred in the 7 X 7 box) minus one cell from the D_4 orbit at L_1 distance 2 from the centre, and all contain a hole. A wider 8 X 8 search at k = 23 returns UNSAT (LRAT certified).\nL146: The hole is therefore a structural feature of a(7), distinguishing this sequence geometrically from the purely numerical match 【19†A350660】. For n in {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9} the optimal container is simply connected.`\nL147: LINKS\nL148: \nL149: `【14†Peter Exley】, <a href=\"https://github.com/pexley-math/oeis-【15†A000000】\">Solver code, data, and figures</a>, GitHub.`\nL150: \nL151: EXAMPLE\nL152: \nL153: `Optimal containers, with one example per illustrated n. Cells are marked '#'; empty cells are '.'.`\nL154: \nL155: `The 1 container with bounding box 3 X 3:`\nL156: \nL157: `. # .`\nL158: \nL159: `# # #`\nL160: \nL161: `. # .`\nL162: \nL163: `The 1 container with bounding box 4 X 4:`\nL164: \nL165: `. . # .`\nL166: \nL167: `. # # .`\nL168: \nL169: `# # # #`\nL170: \nL171: `. # # .`\nL172: \nL173: `The 1 container with bounding box 5 X 5:`\nL174: \nL175: `. . # . .`\nL176: \nL177: `. # # # .`\nL178: \nL179: `# # # # #`\nL180: \nL181: `. # # # .`\nL182: \nL183: `. . # . .`\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nreference request - What is the smallest size of a shape in which all fixed $n$-polyominos can fit? - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/420897/what-is-the-smallest-size-of-a-shape-in-which-all-fixed-n-polyominos-can-fit?noredirect=1)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"lineno\":299}); Total lines: 381\nL261: I guess this is doable without increasing the length too much: none of the neighbors of squares in $B$ are in $B$ by the \"even\" requirement so you can work around them.\nL262: –  【24†a3nm】\nL263: \nL264: Commented May 6, 2022 at 9:24\nL265:   * (iii) The \"Thus $P_{A-a}$\" is because, if a polyomino is in $P_{A-a}$, then its intersection with B is a subset of $(A-a) \\cap B$. (iv) unfortunately I didn't get the very last step; after the \"As when $n$ is big\" I don't see why this can derive the bound that follows and gives the contradiction? Other than that I was able to follow the argument at a high level (I didn't check the precise calculations), I think I got the idea.\nL266: This is very elegant and not at all the kind of techniques I would be used to. Thanks again. :)\nL267: –  【24†a3nm】\nL268: \nL269: Commented May 6, 2022 at 9:26\nL270: \nL271:   * You are welcome! I will edit the things you mention with a bit more detail.\nL272: \nL273: –  【25†Saúl RM】\nL274: \nL275: Commented May 6, 2022 at 11:44\nL276: \nL277:   * 1\nL278: \nL279: Yeah my point is that you can consider the balls for Manhattan distance, which seems to make more intuitive sense given that the polynomino lengths follow the Manhattan distance. But I agree that this works no matter the distance up to changing the constants. Thanks a lot again for the insights!\nL280: \nL281: –  【24†a3nm】\nL282: Commented May 6, 2022 at 14:40\nL283: \nL284:   * 1\nL285: \nL286: You are right, I edited that. I wanted to use the \"normal\" distance but I guess if someone reaches that point of the proof they won't be scared of an $L1$ norm\nL287: \nL288: –  【25†Saúl RM】\nL289: \nL290: Commented May 6, 2022 at 18:44\nL291: \nL292:  |  【32†Show 3 more comments】\nL293: \nL294: [Input]\nL295: \nL296: 4\nL297: \nL298: [Input]\nL299: # Proof outline for $\\Omega(n^{2-\\varepsilon})$\nL300: \nL301: Choose a positive integer $k$ which will determine that $\\varepsilon=\\frac{1}{k}$.\nL302: \nL303: Now consider the problem of embedding the 1 dimensional $k$-block shapes, where the maximum separation is $n$. For example, with $n=4$ and $k=2$ we can do the following:\nL304: \nL305:     `   xx\nL306:       x x\nL307:     x  x\nL308:     x   x\nL309: \nL310:     E EEE\nL311:     `\nL312: \nL313: ^{(Here `E` denotes the embedding and the rows containing `x`'s are the 1D $k$-block shapes)}\nL314: This uses only 4 blocks, instead of the naïve 5 blocks. There is a relatively simple combinatorial lower bound on the size of the minimal embedding, which is roughly $n^{1-\\frac{1}{k}}$.\nL315: \nL316: This bound can be derived from the fact that we have roughly $\\frac{{n \\choose k}}{n}$ unique shapes. If $x$ is the size of the embedding, then $\\frac{{n \\choose k}}{n}\\le{x \\choose k}$ and thus a lower bound for $x$ is $x\\approx n^{1-\\frac{1}{k}}$\nL317: \nL318: Now we just extend this to 2 dimensions.\nL319: For example, we can turn `x x x` ($n=6$,$k=3$) into the following polymino\nL320: \nL321:     `x   x x\nL322:     x   x x\nL323:     xxxxxxx\nL324:     x   x x\nL325:     x   x x\nL326:     x   x x\nL327:     `\nL328: \nL329: Note that the height is equal to $n$. The number of blocks required depends linearly on `n`, since `k` is fixed.\nL330: Now, we know that every row will have an amount of blocks (at least) proportional to $n^{1-1/k}$ and the amount of rows will be proportional to $n$, which means that the number of blocks in the polymino embedding must (roughly) be at least $n\\cdot n^{1-1/k}=n^{2-\\varepsilon}$.\nL331: \nL332: It should be noted that vertical translations are not really useful when restricted to the aforementioned polyminoes. This should be intuitively obvious.\nL333: \nL334: [Button: Cite]\nL335: \nL336: 【38†Improve this answer】\nL337: \nL338: Follow [Input]\nL339: answered Apr 23, 2022 at 15:52\nL340: \nL341: 【39†AnttiP】\nL342: \nL343: 149 4 4 bronze badges\nL344:   * Thanks for the proof! this looks convincing to me except that I don't get how $x≈n^{1−1/k}$ is derived from the previous inequality (which property of the binomial coefficient do you use for this?). Other than that, the one-dimensional non-connected case looks already interesting, it looks like the problem of sparse rulers which is already studied 【40†en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sparse_ruler†en.wikipedia.org】 (and for which we may get more precise asymptotics)\nL345: \nL346: –  【24†a3nm】\nL347: \nL348: Commented Apr 24, 2022 at 15:10\nL349:   * @a3nm Yes, it seems that for $k=2$ the sparse rulers are the optimal embedding. But anyways, the bound can be derived from the fact that for a fixed $k$, ${n \\choose k}$ is a polynomial of degree $k$. Thus $\\frac{{n \\choose k}}{n}$ is approximately a polynomial of degree $k-1$. Now we want to find some function $f(n)$ so that ${f(n) \\choose k}$ grows like a polynomial of degree $k-1$.\nL350: This means that $f(n)$ has to grow like a polynomial of degree ${1-\\frac{1}{k}}$ since if you multiply that by $k$ you get the desired growth rate of $k-1$\nL351: –  【39†AnttiP】\nL352: \nL353: Commented Apr 25, 2022 at 7:17\nL354: \nL355:   * I see, this makes sense, thanks a lot for explaining. And thanks for clarifying that the connection to sparse rulers is only for k=2 -- I don't know if there is an analogous notion for all k-tuples with maximal separation $\\leq n$. (Here as in your post \"maximal separation\" is the maximal total span, i.e., the difference between the min and max)\nL356: \nL357: –  【24†a3nm】\nL358: \nL359: Commented Apr 25, 2022 at 9:56\nL360: \nL361: 【32†Add a comment】  |\nL362: ## You must 【41†log in】 to answer this question.\nL363: \nL364: Start asking to get answers\nL365: \nL366: Find the answer to your question by asking.\nL367: \nL368: 【11†Ask question】\nL369: \nL370: Explore related questions\nL371: \nL372:   * 【17†reference-request】\nL373:   * 【18†co.combinatorics】\nL374:   * 【19†combinatorial-optimization】\nL375:   * 【20†integer-sequences】\nL376:   * 【21†extremal-combinatorics】\nL377: \nL378: See similar questions with these tags.\nL379: \nL380: 【42†Image】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences (OEIS) (https://oeis.org/history?seq=A395495)\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view3\",\"lineno\":20}); Total lines: 207\nL0: 【0†login】\nL1: \nL2: The OEIS is supported by 【1†the many generous donors to the OEIS Foundation†oeisf.org】.\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Winners in the A400K Celebration】\nL5: \nL6: [Input]\nL7: \nL8: [Input]\nL9: \nL10: 【3†Hints】\nL11: \nL12: (Greetings from 【4†The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences】!)\nL13: ## Revision History for 【5†A395495】\nL14: \nL15: (Bold, blue-underlined text is an addition; faded, red-underlined text is a ~~deletion~~.)\nL16: Showing entries 1-10 | 【6†older changes】\nL17: \nL18: 【5†A395495】\nL19: \nL20: Minimum number of cells in a connected polyomino on the square grid that contains every fixed n-omino as a translated subregion.\nL21: (【7†history】; 【5†published version】)\nL22: \nL23: 【8†#16】 by 【9†Sean A. Irvine】 at Mon May 04 21:19:22 EDT 2026\nL24: \nL25: STATUS\nL26: \nL27: `~~proposed~~`\nL28: \nL29: `approved`\nL30: \nL31: 【10†#15】 by 【11†Peter Exley】 at Fri May 01 02:01:50 EDT 2026\nL32: STATUS\nL33: \nL34: `~~editing~~`\nL35: \nL36: `proposed`\nL37: \nL38: 【12†#14】 by 【11†Peter Exley】 at Fri May 01 